-- title:
--   Types of primitives modulo (varpi) at a double point
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, let $j_0 : \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to k$ be a ring homomorphism, where $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ denotes `Zp2 q`, the Witt vectors of the field of $q^2$ elements, and let $X_0$ be a special formal $O_D$-module over $k$ relative to $j_0$: a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $k$ equipped with an action $a \mapsto \mathrm{act}(a)$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ by endomorphisms of $F$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(q)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$ for the Frobenius $\sigma$, such that the two eigenspaces $\mathrm{Lie}_0 = \bigcap_a \ker(\mathrm{Lie}\,\mathrm{act}(a) - j_0(a))$ and $\mathrm{Lie}_1 = \bigcap_a \ker(\mathrm{Lie}\,\mathrm{act}(a) - j_0(\sigma a))$ are complementary invertible submodules of $\mathrm{Lie}\,X_0$, and $\mathrm{act}(q)$ has kernel of degree $q^4$. Assume the double-point condition that the linear part of $\varpi$, viewed as a matrix acting on $\mathrm{Lie}\,X_0$, kills every element of $\mathrm{Lie}_0$ and every element of $\mathrm{Lie}_1$. Let $\psi : \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to k$ be any function and let $g, g'$ be power series in two variables over $k$ with zero constant term such that the additive coboundaries $g(F(X,Y)) - g(X) - g(Y)$ and likewise for $g'$ both lie in the ideal of $k[[X_1,X_2,Y_1,Y_2]]$ generated by the two components of $\varpi(X)$ together with the two components of $\varpi(Y)$, and such that for every $a \in \mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ both $g \circ \mathrm{act}(a) - \psi(a)\,g$ and $g' \circ \mathrm{act}(a) - \psi(a)\,g'$ lie in the ideal generated by the two components of $\varpi$. Then there are scalars $c, c' \in k$, not both zero, with $c\,g + c'\,g' \in (\varpi_1, \varpi_2)$.
--
--   In the deformation theory of special formal $O_D$-modules underlying the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation, the series $g$ satisfying these two conditions are the primitives of type $\psi$ modulo $(\varpi)$; the statement says that at a double point, where the linear part of $\varpi$ vanishes on both $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$-eigenlines of $\mathrm{Lie}\,X_0$, each type contributes at most one line modulo $(\varpi)$. It uses the identification of $(\varpi)$ with $(X_1^q, X_2^q)$ in these coordinates, and feeds the version `exists_smul_add_smul_mem_span_varpi_of_addCoboundary_mem` in which the double-point hypotheses are removed.
